value "\\frac{x^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503864} :answer-field/id #uuid "a740068d-6348-515e-8b5f-4399bec063f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2fcd99b-3b51-589f-9fd0-4c5df6da6a6c" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=3$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Monomial"}]}
